WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.700
The four hundred page PDF nobody can search.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.176
Tables, scans, and cross references break a standard RAG pipeline.

3
00:00:08.176 --> 00:00:10.200
Here's how to fix the ingestion.

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:17.567
Your documents are four hundred page scanned engineering manuals, full of dense tables and diagrams.

5
00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:20.867
A standard chunk and embed pipeline handles them.

6
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:24.900
And it confidently mixes up values from adjacent table rows.

7
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.600
Here's why tables fail.

8
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:34.200
If a chunk holds only two hundred forty volts and fifteen amps, the model no longer knows which is voltage.

9
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:39.490
A fixed size splitter cuts mid row and separates the headers from the values.

10
00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:42.600
Scanned OCR makes it worse by interleaving rows.

11
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.933
The pipeline that works has five steps.

12
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:48.128
First, layout aware parsing.

13
00:00:48.128 --> 00:00:53.067
This sets the quality ceiling, and no better LLM repairs a mangled table.

14
00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:56.400
Tables stay atomic, serialized with their headers.

15
00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.100
Every chunk gets a contextual header, like the section title.

16
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.933
Retrieve the precise row, but send the whole table to the model.

17
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:08.300
And a vision model describes the diagrams, indexed with their captions.

18
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.475
What about see Section 7.

19
00:01:11.475 --> 00:01:11.633
3?

20
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:18.533
Keep a section ID index at ingestion, and when a retrieved chunk points elsewhere, pull that section in too.

21
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:23.333
It's a small graph edge between chunks, where a light knowledge graph earns its keep.

22
00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:25.504
And measure it.

23
00:01:25.504 --> 00:01:31.525
Write thirty spec lookup questions with exact answers, and judge every change on exact match.

24
00:01:31.525 --> 00:01:33.467
For specs, close is worthless.

25
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.933
The trap is tuning chunk size for weeks.

26
00:01:37.233 --> 00:01:38.949
The problem isn't the number.

27
00:01:38.949 --> 00:01:41.967
It's a structure blind splitter, so fix the parser.

28
00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:43.904
Let's recap.

29
00:01:43.904 --> 00:01:46.667
Naive splitters separate headers from values.

30
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:49.033
Parse layout and keep tables whole.

31
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:52.433
Add context headers and retrieve parent and child.

32
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:54.523
And measure with exact match.

33
00:01:54.523 --> 00:01:57.300
Next up: the prompt injection in the invoice.
